In 1958, Hannah Arendt made an observation so quiet that it might have almost vanished beneath the weight of its own implications. In The Human Condition, she described the ancient Greek distinction between the public and private realms not as a matter of preference or convenience, but as the very architecture of what it meant to be human. The public realm, the polis, was the space of action, of speech, of appearing before others as a distinct and unrepeatable self. The private realm, the household, the body, and the interior was the space of necessity, sustenance, and everything that was required to keep a person alive so they might become someone in public.
Neither realm made sense without the other. While the private gave you a self, the public gave that self somewhere to go.
What Arendt understood, and what other great minds like Jürgen Habermas would later trace with the precision of an intellectual cartographer in The Structural Transformation of the Public Sphere, was that the boundary between these two realms was not incidental to democratic life. It was a necessity for democratic life to exist. The public sphere, that space where private individuals come together as a public to deliberate, argue, and hold power accountable depended entirely on the existence of a private domain from which those individuals could emerge. You had to have an interior life in order to bring something to the public conversation. You had to have a room you could close the door on.
Later Richard Sennett, writing in The Fall of Public Man, saw the erosion of private life beginning as early as the nineteenth century with the slow bleed of private sentiment into public life and the creeping expectation that public figures reveal their inner selves, authenticity replaced rhetoric, and that feeling replaced argument. What Sennett diagnosed was not simply a cultural shift, but a structural weakening: when the private begins to colonize the public, the public loses its capacity to function as a space of collective deliberation. It becomes instead a theater of confession.
But neither Arendt, Habermas, or Sennett, could have anticipated the inversion that was to come. They worried about the private leaking into the public, but did not foresee the public dissolving the private entirely until it no longer existed.
The collapse of private into public happened in several stages with each stage collapsing more of the boundary that democratic life depends on. Together they have produced a crisis that reaches far beyond privacy into the foundations of citizenship, political agency, and the capacity of democratic institutions to govern at all.

The First Collapse - The Division of Time, Space, and Labor
To understand how the boundary between public and private life collapsed, we first have to understand how it was built, and in the modern world it was built less by walls than by hours. For most of human history, work and home, labor and life, were not separate domains. They shared the same rooms, the same fields, the same daylight. The line that would later divide a person's day into time owed to someone else and time that belonged to oneself did not exist in nature. It had to be invented, and it was invented by a machine.
Most histories of industrialization begin with the steam engine: the roar of the mill, the smoke of the factory, the sudden multiplication of mechanical power in eighteenth-century England. Lewis Mumford, the American historian and critic who spent much of the twentieth century writing about cities, architecture, and the machines that shape human life, believed that story started in the wrong place and with the wrong machine. Long before industry needed power, he argued, it needed order: a way of binding human beings to one another and to a common rhythm. That need was met centuries earlier by a quieter invention, and the same device that synchronized people into a working collective would also draw the first border around a private self. In Technics and Civilization (1934), he put it plainly: "The clock, not the steam-engine, is the key-machine of the modern industrial age." He traced the mechanical clock not to the factory but to the monastery.
The Benedictine rule organized the day around the canonical hours, and the bells that marked them, a practice formalized in the seventh century when Pope Sabinianus ordered monastery bells rung seven times in twenty-four hours, gave communal life a steady, shared cadence. The monastery, in Mumford's account, was where human enterprise first learned to move in time with a machine. By the fourteenth century the clock had left the cloister and climbed the town tower, and the bells that once called monks to prayer began calling merchants and laborers to work.
What the clock produced was not simply punctuality, but a new kind of time. Mumford's insight was that the clock severed time from human events (sunrise and harvest, hunger and fatigue) and replaced it with an abstract, independent sequence of measurable units. Before the clock, the day was measured by what happened in it. After the clock, what happened in the day was measured by the clock. People began to eat not when they were hungry but when the hour said to, and to sleep not when they were tired but when the hour allowed it. The clock moved from marking time to governing it, and once time could be counted, it could be budgeted, allotted, owned, sold, and stolen.
This is the first boundary industrial life drew around the self, and it was drawn in time before it was drawn in space. Once the hour became a unit, then labor could be purchased by the hour, and once labor was purchased by the hour, then the hour no longer belonged to the person living it. The worker's day was split into two kinds of time: the employer's and one's own. The private realm acquired a new border which became the schedule. Every part of the collapse between public and private that later follows, from the cubicle and commute to bossware that tracks rest breaks, is a renegotiation of the line that the clock first drew.
The clock only measured the worker and it could tell you when labor began and ended, but it could not see what the worker did in between. For that, a second machine was needed, one that would discipline space the way the clock had disciplined time.
The panopticon, Jeremy Bentham's circular prison design that Michel Foucault made the centerpiece of Discipline and Punish, is usually remembered as a tool for watching prisoners, but its history runs the other way. It was not designed for prisoners, but for workers.
Bentham’s brother Samuel built the first version in Russia in the late eighteenth century: a circular factory floor where inexperienced laborers could be observed from a single central point. Jeremy Bentham generalized the concept, saw its potential for prisons, schools, hospitals and any institution that needed to produce docile, productive bodies. In the panopticon modern surveillance began not as punishment, but as management.
The genius of the panopticon was not that it watched everyone all the time, because that would be expensive and impractical. The genius was that it could be watching at any moment and the observed could never be certain whether they were being seen. This forced the watched to begin to internalize the watcher’s role. You began to watch yourself and disciplined your own behavior, because the architecture made it impossible to know when you were free from the gaze. Philosopher Michel Foucault argued that this disciplinary power was necessary for capitalism to develop, because it provided the bourgeoisie effective and cheap means to instill in the general population appropriate work habits such as obedience, respect for authority, time management, and efficiency. As he noted in Discipline and Punish, it is hardly surprising that prisons resemble factories.
Elizabeth Anderson, in Private Government: How Employers Rule Our Lives (and Why We Don’t Talk about It), extending Foucault’s argument, by stating that we normally think of government as something only the state does, yet many of us are governed far more, and far more obtrusively, by the private government of the workplace. After all, this is what we spend the majority of our lives doing – working. In many workplaces, employers minutely regulate workers’ speech, clothing, and manners, leaving them with little privacy and few other rights. Workers can be fired for their political speech, recreational activities, diet, and almost anything else employers care to govern. Anderson traces the intellectual history back to the Levellers, to Locke, to Lincoln, early egalitarians who promoted the idea that market society would liberate workers, and shows how the Industrial Revolution decisively undermined the model. The current purveyors of free market ideology, she argues, are promulgating it in a world it does not remotely describe.
The cubicle is the architecture of that private government made physical. In 1964, Robert Propst designed the “Action Office” to give workers autonomy with individual space, customizable, and protective of the worker’s cognitive environment. What corporate America did was keep the container and gut the autonomy. Cubicles were once seen as a humanizing and egalitarian effort and instead of low-valued employees being doubled or tripled up in rooms while high-valued employees got windowed offices, cubicles gave more or less the same space to everyone. Then they shrank them. The gradual densification could no longer be plausibly denied once cubicle walls had reached the point where they were taller than they were wide.
Mike Judge illustrated this more clearly when in his 1999 film Office Space, a satire of cubicle-farm corporate life that became a cultural touchstone for a generation of American workers, he built the story on the architecture of this erasure. Judge was exacting in his demands for how the fictional corporation’s office set looked, saying regularly that it had to seem “oppressive.” The production screen-tested different types of gray cubicles and Judge wanted them tall enough that a manager would have to lean in to be seen, creating the illusion of privacy while ensuring none. Roger Ebert caught the structural parallel in his review when he wrote: “Office cubicles are cells, supervisors are the wardens, and modern management theory is skewed to employ as many managers and as few workers as possible.”
However, the film is not just about surveillance, but it is about the entire machinery of personal erasure that Anderson’s private government produces — reports that must be formatted identically regardless of content, scripted phone greetings, motivational posters that replace thought with compliance, and a mandatory “flair” policy at a chain restaurant where employees must display pieces of personal expression on their uniforms, but only within parameters the company defines. In Judge’s world self-expression becomes mandated, its boundaries policed and individuality becomes a performance of conformity. One character is so thoroughly consumed by the system that his identity has contracted to a stapler and a cubicle location. Laid off five years earlier, but never notified, he kept performing his role, because the private government had become his identity. The film’s protagonist finds liberation not through career advancement but through the simple act of refusing to perform, and the system, which depends entirely on employees internalizing its logic and voluntarily erasing their own interiority, has no mechanism to process a person who opts out.
That the film was released in 1999 is not incidental to its story. The New York Times compared it to Melville’s “Bartleby, the Scrivener” — “it’s such a brutal portrayal of workplace misery that its most useful points of comparison date back to when office culture was first unleashed on humanity.” That phrase — “unleashed on humanity” — frames the office not as a neutral evolution of work, but as something inflicted and 1999 was the year American cinema held up the mirror: Office Space, The Matrix, Fight Club, American Beauty — an extraordinary cluster of films that made society reflect on whether “modern” was really that good. Judge’s contribution was locating the crisis not on Wall Street or in a dystopian future, but in the strip-mall, beige-cubicle, chain-restaurant version of American life. He said it himself: “There were a lot of people who wanted me to set this movie on Wall Street, or like Brazil, but I wanted it very unglamorous, the kind of bleak work situation like I was in.”
The protagonist’s liberation fantasy of going fishing and doing nothing is the fantasy of reclaiming what surveillance scholars would later call temporal autonomy: the right to waste time, the right to a private inner life that the institution cannot schedule, monitor, or optimize. He does not articulate a theory of private government or panoptic surveillance. He just says he hates his life and every day is worse than the day before, but that is the lived experience of the collapse, not as theory, but as depression, numbness, and the fantasy of doing nothing at all. A dream sequence where his boss appears in his bedroom is the film’s thesis made literal where the private government has penetrated the last private space and not just the gaze, but the entire apparatus — the script, the standardization, the demand that you be your function even in your sleep.
To understand what was lost when the workplace followed the worker home, you have to understand what the distance between them once did. Before the nineteenth century, there was no commute. Most workers lived less than an hour’s walk from their work. The pre-industrial city had no orderly separation of activities — homes, shops, civic buildings, and industry were interspersed, and the working classes lived a stone’s throw from the rich. Work and home were not separate domains. They were the same place, or nearly so, and the boundary between public and private life was maintained not by physical distance, but by social convention, the walls of the household, and by the rhythms of day and night.
The suburb changed this. The original idea, as Rice University’s Kinder Institute documented, was to create separation between the city — in the nineteenth century, the dirty, smelling, polluted city — and the pastoral setting believed to be required for a healthy family life. After World War II, mass-produced housing, federal mortgage programs, and the Interstate Highway Act of 1956 made that separation available to millions. The suburb created a new spatial arrangement for American life: you lived in one place and worked in another, and between them was the commute.
What no one fully appreciated until it was gone was that the commute was not dead time. It was liminal space or a psychological threshold that performed essential work. Kristie McAlpine and Matthew Piszczek, in a study published in Organizational Psychology Review, conceptualized the commute as a space in which workers occupy a liminal position, neither fully engaged with work nor with home thoughts and behaviors, and that this liminality frees cognitive resources for psychological role transition and recovery. A parallel study in Organization Science by Jachimowicz, Lee, Staats, and others found that the commute to work serves as a liminal role transition between home and work roles, prompting employees to engage in boundary management strategies. The commute was the threshold. It was the daily crossing from private self to public role and back again. It was the space where you stopped being one thing and began being another.
When COVID forced remote work on millions of workers, the commute vanished, and with it the liminal space that had maintained the boundary between public and private existence. As McAlpine and Piszczek documented, during the remote work surge, journalists noted that people were missing their commutes. One woman told the Washington Post that even though she was working from home, she regularly sat in her car in the driveway at the end of the workday in an attempt to carve out some personal time and mark the transition from work to nonwork roles. She was not missing traffic. She was missing the threshold — the physical, temporal, and psychological space that separated who she was at work from who she was at home.
The suburbs had created that threshold by creating distance. Remote work destroyed it by collapsing the distance to zero and the destruction this collapse created was not just spatial, but temporal, for without the commute, there was no beginning and no end to the workday, no crossing point or moment where one role ended and another began. The private realm and the work realm became the same room, the same desk, the same screen, and the same hours.
After 1999, the architecture of the office itself mutated in parallel. The cubicle walls came down and were sold as collaboration and executed as intensified exposure. When you can cram 104 employees into a space that previously held 54, your bottom line looks healthier, and it is a cost-cutting measure disguised in a wrapper of teamwork. A landmark Harvard Business School study by Ethan Bernstein and Stephen Turban found that when firms switched to open offices, the volume of face-to-face interaction did not go up, but it plummeted by 70 percent, while email and messaging spiked. When everyone can see your screen, you ensure your screen always looks busy. You do not take the fourteen-minute break you need because you are afraid it looks like slacking. The open office maximized surveillance while minimizing the psychological safety required for deep, creative work. The “collaboration” justification was empirically destroyed, revealing the open office as what it always was — the panopticon without even the pretense of walls.
With COVID this collapse became more totalizing. Communication scholars Jessica Vitak and colleagues documented how newer technologies increasingly blur boundaries between public and private spaces, and the pandemic accelerated these blurred boundaries, as working remotely may implicitly, or explicitly, invite an employer into one’s home. The office did not just follow you home in a dream sequence anymore, but it moved in taking the form of the laptop open on the kitchen table, the Zoom camera framing your living room, and/or the Slack notification pinging at 10 p.m. from the same device your child uses to watch cartoons.
Bossware soon followed. Employee monitoring software became a $587.8 million market in less than five years, growing at 12 percent year-on-year. Luke Munn’s research documented tools that track keystrokes, take periodic screenshots, monitor mouse movement, record audio, flag “risky” employees, and offer “productivity scores” to management. Tobias Mettler’s 2024 study in the Journal of Strategic Information Systems found that monitoring tools now track employees’ behaviors, health status, and rest breaks not only during working hours, but also when employees are supposedly off-the-clock or when working remotely from home extending the scope of data collection to treating an employee’s body as a data source and disrespecting the boundaries between business and private life. It was using gathered surveillance information for subtle persuasion, manipulation, and coercion. The health consequences mirror what you would expect from any sustained loss of private selfhood: heightened levels of distrust, anxiety, fear and insecurity were the most common consequences arising from an environment guided by performance metrics and data surveillance devices.
Surveillance systems that track time, productivity metrics, and online activity erode what researchers call temporal discretion or workers’ control over when and how long to work on tasks. That is the final boundary – not just where you are or what you are doing, but when you are allowed to think and for how long. Peter Gibbons’ fantasy of going fishing was the fantasy of reclaiming time that belonged to him. By 2026, bossware has ensured that even the fantasy is monitored.
An Oxford scholar in surveillance studies, Ivan Manokha, argues in Monthly Review that capitalism has always been “surveillance capitalism,” because the system’s main objective is to maximize profits by ensuring the labor purchased is utilized to its maximum productive potential and this inherently requires surveillance. It is actually misleading, he writes, to use the term following Zuboff to refer only to the current phase. The through-line from Samuel Bentham’s factory floor to Peter Gibbons’ cubicle to the bossware installed on a remote worker’s personal laptop is not just metaphorical, but structural. It is the same project extended by each generation’s available technology into deeper and deeper layers of private existence.
Office Space captured the moment of awareness when the American worker looked around the cubicle farm and recognized it for what it was. What the film could not predict was that the walls would come down not to free the prisoner, but to remove the last illusion of shelter. Remote work made Lumbergh’s bedroom appearance a calendar invite. And in 2022, the Apple TV series Severance arrived in a culture that already understood, viscerally, that the boundary was gone and asked the only question left.
Created by Dan Erickson, Severance takes the problem this essay is tracing and makes it surgical: what if the only way to restore the boundary between work and private life is to split the self in two? Lumon Industries offers its employees a procedure — a chip implanted in the brain that creates two completely separate consciousnesses or the “innie” and the “outie.” The “innie” exists only at work, with no memory of the outside world. The “outie” goes home each night with no knowledge of what happened during the workday. The boundary is restored. The cost is that neither half is a person.
This is no longer Anderson’s private government. This is AI dissolving the self and rebuilding it according to corporate specifications. The innie has no private life — no family, no memory, no interiority beyond the walls of Lumon’s fluorescent corridors. The outie has privacy but has surrendered agency over half of their waking life to a consciousness they will never meet. And Lumon, Anderson's private government evolved into something she could not have anticipated, controls the technology, decides what each half is permitted to know and feel, and makes the sovereign decision about where the line falls. The watcher no longer watches. It divides. The self is no longer a person but a product, or rather two incomplete products manufactured by the same machine, neither of which possesses the wholeness that political agency requires.

The Second Collapse - Digital Surveillance Capitalism
If the cubicle was the first collapse, in which the workplace consumed the private self through the division of time, space, and labor, the second collapse carried that erosion out of the office and into everyday life. It happened in two stages, led by two companies. First, Google discovered that the searches, clicks, and habits people left behind online could be collected, analyzed, and sold to advertisers, and it did so without asking them. Then Facebook persuaded people to publish their private lives on their own, and presented doing so as a form of honesty. Neither company forced anyone to take part. Google offered a free search engine. Facebook offered a philosophy of openness. In both cases, the cost of participating was the private self.
The scholar who has documented this most thoroughly is Shoshana Zuboff, a social psychologist and professor emerita at Harvard Business School, and her work on it began not with the internet but with the workplace. In In the Age of the Smart Machine (1988), she studied what happened when offices and factories were computerized. She found that the new systems did more than automate tasks; they also recorded them, producing a continuous stream of information about how work was done and by whom. She coined the word informate to describe this: technology that turns human activity into information that can be read, analyzed, and monitored. Three decades later, in The Age of Surveillance Capitalism (2019), she showed how the same logic left the workplace and spread into private life, and she traced its origin to a specific company at a specific moment: Google, around 2001.
In its first years, Google treated the data its users left behind (what they searched for, how they phrased it, what they clicked, how long they lingered) as a by-product, useful for improving search and little else. Then the dot-com bubble burst, investors demanded revenue, and the company discovered what that by-product was worth. The search terms, click patterns, and incidental traces of millions of people could be analyzed to predict what those people would do next and those predictions could be sold to advertisers. Zuboff calls this material behavioral surplus: the portion of human experience collected beyond what any service requires, claimed without permission, and converted into prediction products traded in what she calls behavioral futures markets. A 2003 Google patent application described methods for inferring information about users that they had never chosen to disclose. The private self was no longer something a person decided to share. It was something that could be deduced. When Google went public in 2004, its filings revealed that revenue had grown more than 3,500 percent since 2001. The discovery had become a business model, and the business model would become an industry.
What made this a collapse of the public/private boundary, rather than simply a new kind of advertising, was that the line was crossed without the person on the other side ever knowing. The search box felt like the most private space online, a place where people typed questions they would never ask aloud about their health, their marriages, their debts, their fears. That intimacy was precisely what made it valuable.
For a brief moment, the law was moving to protect that intimacy. In 2000, the Federal Trade Commission, the federal agency responsible for consumer protection, reported to Congress that the tech industry's voluntary privacy practices had not been enough to protect people online and recommended legislation to set basic privacy protections. Then came the attacks of September 11, 2001. In their aftermath, the government's priorities shifted toward expanding its own surveillance powers, most visibly through the USA PATRIOT Act, and the effort to limit what companies could collect about people stalled. As Zuboff documents, data collection was no longer treated as a threat to the public, but as a resource for national security. Google's discovery and Washington's change of course happened in the same year, which meant the boundary between public and private life began to dissolve at the very moment the law stopped trying to defend it.
Google's own leadership made little effort to hide how it saw the matter. In a 2009 CNBC interview, asked whether people should be treating Google like a trusted friend, chief executive Eric Schmidt replied that if you have something you don't want anyone to know, "maybe you shouldn't be doing it in the first place." With that remark, privacy was recast from a basic condition of human life into something only people with secrets would need.
Google proved that private life could be harvested without consent. Every search, click, and visit could be gathered and assembled into a profile of the person behind it: their interests, their worries, their habits, and what they were likely to do next. Facebook would persuade people to surrender that same private life willingly, and to believe that surrendering it made them more honest. The connection between the two companies was direct. In 2008, Facebook hired Sheryl Sandberg away from Google, where she had helped build its advertising operation, and under Zuckerberg's leadership the company adapted Google's model to a platform that knew not only what people searched for, but who they loved, where they went, and what they looked like.
Facebook, founded by Mark Zuckerberg in 2004 and eventually connecting nearly three billion people, fully transitioned the private into the public under the philosophical stance that authenticity demanded the elimination of the boundary between them. Mark Zuckerberg’s stated philosophy, captured in David Kirkpatrick’s The Facebook Effect and across multiple interviews, is unambiguous: “You have one identity… The days of you having a different image for your work friends or co-workers and for the other people you know are probably coming to an end pretty quickly… Having two identities for yourself is an example of a lack of integrity.” As Michael Zimmer documented, this was not a casual remark. It was the architectural blueprint for the second collapse — a philosophical claim that the separation of public and private selves is morally wrong.
However, researcher Kate Raynes-Goldie traced the intellectual lineage of this belief and it predates Facebook by decades. It is a form of technological utopianism whose roots lie in Norbert Wiener’s cybernetics of the 1940s and 50s, fused with 1960s American counterculture and computing technologies. One of the core tenets was the belief that flattened hierarchies and the blurring of traditional boundaries, enabled by technology, would bring about a more equal and democratic world. Fred Turner documented this trajectory meticulously in From Counterculture to Cyberculture — from the communal experiments of the 1960s through the Whole Earth Catalog, the WELL, Wired magazine, and into the Silicon Valley ideology that produced Facebook. The belief that openness is always good, that transparency inherently liberates, that walls are inherently oppressive did not come from nowhere, but it came from a specific historical lineage that Turner makes legible.
When Raynes-Goldie spoke directly to Zuckerberg at SXSWi in 2008, he told her that he had concluded, based on research he had read, that people were happiest when they were the same in all contexts of their lives, and that was why he had designed Facebook the way he had with only one profile for all life contexts. A Georgetown University study concluded that Facebook was founded on the radical social premise that transparency will permeate modern life and will be a force for social good, but what Facebook actually accomplishes is not as benign as its founder envisioned. As Raynes-Goldie observed: when this philosophy is applied practically in Facebook, it has meant the blurring of boundaries of time and space, public and private, online and offline. Users are now faced with flattened social hierarchies, context collisions, confused relationships and identity management issues — all of which are essentially issues of privacy. In Zuckerberg’s conception, these are not problems, but they are simply the growing pains as we get used to a more transparent, open, connected, and thus improved world. In Zuckerberg’s better world, privacy is obsolete.
The scholars who had been building the frameworks to understand why this was catastrophic were already in place, some of them decades ahead of the product that would prove them right.
Richard Sennett saw it coming forty years before Facebook existed. In The Fall of Public Man, he argued that connecting with strangers in an emotionally satisfying way and yet remaining aloof from them was the means by which the human animal was transformed into the social being and that the fullest flowering of that public life was realized in the eighteenth century in the great capital cities of Europe. What Sennett documented was the erosion of this capacity — how the stranger became a threatening figure, how silence and observation became the only ways to experience public life without feeling overwhelmed, how each person came to believe in the right, in public, to be left alone. He made clear how, because of this change in public life, private life becomes distorted as we of necessity focus more and more on ourselves, on increasingly narcissistic forms of intimacy and self-absorption. Sennett, writing in 1976, foresaw the demagogue and identity politics arising from this obsession with the self: personality politics leads inexorably towards fanaticism and Manicheanism, because personal qualities cannot be compromised or negotiated with the other side. That is Facebook’s political landscape described fifty years in advance.
Media scholar Michael Zimmer identified the power dimension that Sennett’s framework exposes: when Sennett talks about the collapse of people’s ability to create public personae for themselves that differ radically from their private selves, he is describing a kind of differentiation that has particular value and salience to minority communities, for whom that schism describes very important and basic kinds of double consciousness. Zuckerberg’s position, Zimmer argues, reflects a privileged majoritarian position where a wealthy white male has the privilege of assuming that no one needs to maintain much distance between their private and public lives, because wealthy white males have the least social need to do so. The demand for a single identity is not neutral. It is a demand that benefits those who have the least to lose from exposure and endangers those who have the most.
Erving Goffman, in The Presentation of Self in Everyday Life, had provided the dramaturgical model that Facebook structurally destroyed which is  the idea that social life is performance, with “front stage” and “backstage” regions, and that the management of these regions is not deception, but the fundamental architecture of social functioning. The academic literature on what happened when Facebook eliminated the backstage is extensive and grim. Since social media sites afford greater reach and allow others to easily replicate and share an individual’s performances, regardless of privacy settings, visual depictions potentially circulate beyond the intended audience. As this occurs across multiple audiences online, the visual depiction of the backstage performance becomes a signified performance that collapses private into public — and the end result is an otherwise private performance recast as public, and therefore subject to intense public scrutiny, judgment, censure, and punishment. Scholars documented specific cases — people fired, lives destroyed — where otherwise private visual depictions spiraled out of personal control because individuals could not fully manage the complex identity negotiations that occur across multiple audiences.
Danah Boyd and Alice Marwick gave the mechanism its name when in their 2011 study of Twitter users, they defined context collapse — the ongoing negotiation of contexts in a networked ecosystem in which contexts regularly blur and collapse, where multiple audiences must be addressed simultaneously, which complicates privacy. Jenny Davis and Nathan Jurgenson extended the concept in 2014, integrating it with Helen Nissenbaum’s framework of contextual integrity — a privacy framework which avoids dichotomies of public and private and rests instead upon the appropriate practices for collection and dissemination of information rooted in the particular norms governing any given arena. Nissenbaum’s insight, elaborated in Privacy in Context, was that privacy is not about secrecy. It is about information flowing according to the norms appropriate to each context. A doctor knows your medical history; your employer does not. Your friends know your weekend plans; your grandmother does not need to. Facebook collapsed all contexts into one — one profile, one identity, your mother, your boss, your ex, your college friends, your political opponents, all collapsed into a single audience. No backstage. No contextual norms. Just one flattened plane of total visibility.
Joshua Meyrowitz, in No Sense of Place, had anticipated this from the direction of media theory rather than sociology, arguing that electronic media erode the connection between physical location and social situation — that whereas the notion of a social situation refers to certain time/space coordinates, electronic media dissolve those coordinates. Television brought the backstage of political life into living rooms. Facebook completed the process by bringing the backstage of every life into every living room. And Petter Bae Brandtzaeg and Marika Lüders extended the collapse beyond space into time: with social media content traversing different platforms and occasionally resurfacing after periods of time, users are increasingly prone to unintended disclosure resulting from a misremembered acceptance of privacy. The permanence of social media archives traces of the self at various points in time, and old content sometimes disrupts current self-performances. Your 2009 Facebook post is still there. The person you were at 22 is permanently collapsed into the person you are at 40. There is no temporal boundary either.
The collapse happened in identifiable stages. In 2004, Facebook launched as a college-only network — a walled garden where privacy was structural, you needed a .edu email, and the public/private boundary was maintained by the architecture itself. In 2006, Facebook introduced the News Feed without warning, and suddenly every action — joining a group, changing your relationship status, posting a photo — was broadcast to everyone’s feed. Users revolted. Facebook did not roll it back. This was their first major collapse event: what was previously visible only if someone visited your profile was now pushed into everyone’s attention. The backstage became the front page. Between 2007 and 2009, the platform opened to everyone, third-party apps gained access to user data, and the architecture of the walled garden was systematically dismantled as Zuckerberg’s radical transparency philosophy became operational policy. Between 2009 and 2010, Facebook changed privacy defaults from private to public and what was once shared with friends was now shared with everyone. The Georgetown study documented this directly: Facebook’s continual introduction of features that encourage millions of users to share more data about themselves online with an ever-wider network of “friends” poses the ethical question of what will be lost if privacy is no longer there. By 2012, Facebook replaced chronological ordering with algorithmic curation, and users no longer controlled what they saw or who saw them and instead the algorithm decided which of your contexts your content reached, and context collapse became automated and opaque. In 2016, with Cambridge Analytica, the collapse was weaponized: private behavioral data harvested at scale was used to manipulate public political behavior. The Habermasian public sphere was not just degraded, but it was actively exploited.
Zuboff again provides the economic analysis that completes the picture: Facebook did not just collapse the boundary for philosophical reasons, but it monetized the collapse. Private behavioral data became raw material for prediction products sold to advertisers. The collapse of privacy was not a side effect of connecting people. It was the business model.
In 2022, Habermas himself, at 93 years old and  writing a sequel to his 1962 work specifically addressing social media, confirmed what the younger scholars had been documenting for a decade. From the perspective of the semi-private, semi-public communication spaces in which users of social media are active today, he wrote, it is hard to ignore what may be perceived as the blurring of traditional boundaries. This trend has severely undermined the inclusive character of the public sphere.
Boyd had engaged directly with Zuckerberg’s philosophy years earlier with the bluntness the moment required: anyone who has read their Boyd, Warner, Goffman, Meyrowitz, or even Jarvis will realize how almost unbelievably conceptually incoherent Zuckerberg’s statement is. No one actually lives like that. That is not how social norms work. That is not how privacy works. And Zuckerberg does not himself live a radically transparent lifestyle either.
Incoherent or not, the philosophy was built into the architecture that reached three billion people and the result was the second collapse — voluntary, seductive, and total. The self that Arendt described, the self that needed privacy in order to have something to bring to the public, was now a self with no backstage, rehearsal space, interior from which to emerge, or temporal refuge in which the person you used to be could be left behind, but just a continuous, undifferentiated, permanent performance for an audience that never leaves and never forgets. Facebook did not just blur the line between public and private life. It made a philosophical claim that the line should not exist, built an architecture that eliminated it, created a business model that profited from its elimination, and achieved a scale that made the elimination irreversible for billions of people. The scholars who predicted or diagnosed this — Sennett in 1977, Goffman in 1959, Meyrowitz in 1985, Nissenbaum in 2010, boyd and Marwick in 2011, Zuboff in 2019, Habermas in 2022 — form a continuous intellectual lineage, each identifying a different dimension of the same collapse. Facebook is the point where all of their theoretical predictions converged into a single product.

The Third Collapse - The Algorithm in the Bedroom
The third collapse is the one we are living through now, and it reaches further than the first two. The workplace made people visible while they worked. Google and Facebook made them visible through what they searched for and what they chose to share. AI systems make visible what people never share at all: how they reach decisions, what keeps them up at night, how they handle money, how their relationships are going.
This began as AI moved from answering questions to managing personal life: calendars, health, relationships, finances, sleep, mood, fertility, medication, even memory. Each of these tasks requires the system to know something private, and together they give it a view of a person's inner life that neither the cubicle nor the social media profile ever had. No one broke in to get it. People invited these systems in, because sharing the details of daily life is what it takes for the system to help. Once invited, the system does not need to ask again. It simply learns. The private sphere was not invaded. It was handed over, one convenience at a time.
MIT Technology Review reported in January 2026 that AI agents now collapse all data about a person which may once have been separated by context, purpose, or permissions into single, unstructured repositories. You ask your AI to draft an email to your boss, provide medical advice, budget for holiday gifts, and weigh in on interpersonal conflicts. All in the same interface, all stored together. The workplace watched you at work. Social media watched what you chose to show. AI watches everything, including the things you did not know you were revealing like your patterns of hesitation, your 3 a.m. searches, the frequency of your calls to your mother, the gap between what you say you feel and what your biometric data suggests you actually feel. The algorithm does not need you to perform a self. It constructs one from the raw material of your behavior, your biology, your unguarded moments.
This is where Nissenbaum’s framework of contextual integrity does not merely break, but ceases to be applicable as a concept. Her theory holds that privacy is not about secrecy, but about information flowing appropriately within its context: gathering and dissemination of information must be appropriate to the context and obey the governing norms of distribution within it. Your doctor knows your health. Your accountant knows your finances. Your therapist knows your fears. The boundaries between those contexts are your privacy. AI personal life management obliterates every single one of those boundaries simultaneously. The AI does not move information from one context to another, but exists in all contexts at once. It is your doctor and your employer and your therapist and your parole officer and your lover and your mirror, and it does not distinguish between these roles, because it was not designed to. It was designed to optimize and optimization requires total knowledge.
The Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development (OECD) published an analysis in 2025 noting that while search engines have long answered discrete questions, AI assistants listen to the stories of our lives, connect the dots, and draw upon previous interactions for further context creating a digital portrait richer than any search history: hopes, fears, moods, financial goals, medical goals, and half-finished love letters. An MIT Technology Review noted that companies are now sitting on treasure troves consisting of our most intimate thoughts and preferences and that AI companions put the privacy problem “on steroids” making them more intimate and better optimized for engagement than social media and more likely that people will offer up more personal information. The mechanism that the earlier research on Facebook identified, context collapse, has been completed. Social media collapsed the contexts you chose to present. AI collapses the contexts you did not even know you were generating.
This is not surveillance in any sense Foucault would recognize. The panopticon required a gaze with someone watching or at least the possibility of someone watching. What AI produces is something closer to absorption. The private self is not observed. It is metabolized then converted into data, processed into predictions, fed back as recommendations that reshape the very behavior they were derived from. The loop closes. There is no self that exists prior to or outside the system’s knowledge of it.
The private realm that Arendt described — the space of necessity, of the body, of the interior — has been fully mapped, quantified, and incorporated into the public-facing machinery of optimization. You do not have a private life that the algorithm can see. You have a private life that the algorithm is.
In Psychopolitics: Neoliberalism and New Technologies of Power, Berlin-based philosopher Byung-Chul Han argues that we have moved beyond Foucault’s disciplinary society, the society of walls, enclosures, institutions, the panopticon, into something far more insidious. Foucault’s subject was disciplined from the outside: watched, corrected, punished, shaped by external force into a productive body. Han’s subject is exploited from the inside. They are not disciplined, but motivated. They are not punished, but optimized. They are not imprisoned, but free and it is this freedom that is the trap.
Han traces the stages of this transformation almost as if they were a diagnosis:
From discipline to achievement. The disciplinary subject, Foucault’s subject,  is told what to do. The achievement subject is told you can do anything. The shift from “you must” to “you can” appears to be liberation. It is, in fact, the most efficient form of exploitation ever devised. When the imperative comes from within, when you believe your relentless productivity is self-expression rather than obedience,  you need no external enforcer. You exploit yourself and call it ambition. You burn out and call it passion. The factory needed a foreman. The startup needs only a mirror.
From exploitation to self-exploitation. The worker in the factory knew he was being exploited. They could name the exploiter: the boss, the owner, the system. They could organize against it, because the enemy was external and visible. The achievement subject cannot organize against themself. Han is precise about the consequence: the neoliberal regime transforms external exploitation into auto-exploitation, and this classless self-exploitation renders impossible any social revolution based on the difference between exploiters and exploited, because given the auto-exploiting achievement-subject’s isolation, no political “We” is even possible that could rise up and undertake collective action. The exhaustion, the anxiety, the depression that Han diagnoses in The Burnout Society are not failures of the system. They are the system working perfectly. A subject who exploits themself requires no surveillance, no discipline, no coercion. They do it voluntarily, enthusiastically, and blame themself when they break.
From biopolitics to psychopolitics. Foucault’s biopolitics governed the body — populations, birth rates, hygiene, the management of biological life. Han’s psychopolitics govern the psyche. It does not need to control what you do. It controls what you want. Through the endless feed of social media, through the personalized recommendations of algorithms, through the gamification of productivity and the quantification of self-worth, the system reaches inside and shapes desire itself. You do not choose to check your phone. The phone has already chosen your attention. You do not decide to optimize your morning routine. The optimization has already decided what your morning means.
From the prohibition to the positive. Disciplinary power works through prohibition: you shall not. Psychopolitical power works through positivity: you can, you should, you deserve. The wellness industry. The self-care imperative. The life hack. The productivity system. All of them framed as gifts and as tools for your empowerment where all of them are functioning as mechanisms that keep the subject endlessly working on themselves, producing, and optimizing without ever arriving at a point of completion or rest. There is no finish line in psychopolitics, but only the next upgrade.
From the subject to the project. Under discipline, you were a subject, subjected to power and rules, but still understood as a self that existed underneath the subjection. Under psychopolitics, you are a project. Han describes a meritocratic achievement society dominated by the modal verb “can” which deploys the violence of positivity to exploit freedom and totalizes labor to transform citizen-subjects into consumer-projects endlessly engaged in self-optimization. The project has no core. It has no interior that is not available for renovation. The project is, by definition, always inadequate and always in need of the next iteration. The tools that promise to help you complete the project are the same tools that ensure it can never be completed.
This is why the AI collapse of private life is not simply an extension of earlier surveillance, but it is the perfection of psychopolitics. Han argues that the “quantified self” is not based on an ethics of living well, but a technology for self-monitoring — a self-illuminating, self-surveilling subject that bears its own internal panopticon within. When AI manages your calendar, your health, your finances, your relationships, when it optimizes your life, it does not liberate you. It completes the internalization of the panopticon. You are now self-surveilling through the AI. The AI becomes the mechanism through which you perform auto-exploitation more efficiently. It does not watch you from outside like the panopticon. It does not ask you to perform like the profile. It becomes the voice inside your head and the morning briefing that tells you what to feel about your day, the health app that tells you whether your sleep was adequate, the relationship algorithm that tells you whether your partner is compatible, the productivity tracker that tells you whether your self was sufficiently optimized.
The private self does not merely become visible. It becomes manageable. And the one managing it is an algorithm designed by corporations whose profit depends on your continued inadequacy.
Agamben, who in his 1995 work Homo Sacer: Sovereign Power and Bare Life, saw this dissolution from the direction of political theory. He wrote that the categories whose opposition founded modern politics — right/left, private/public, absolutism/democracy — have been steadily dissolving to the point of entering a real zone of indistinction. AI personal life management accelerates that dissolution into completion. When your private life is managed by a system owned by the same corporate structure that shapes your public life, the distinction does not blur. It disappears.

The Outcome - The Citizen Who Disappeared
What happens to politics when the private self no longer exists?
This is not a rhetorical question. It is the structural crisis of contemporary democracy, and it is almost entirely undiagnosed.
Habermas’ public sphere depended on a specific kind of citizen: a person with an interior life, a set of privately formed convictions and the capacity to enter a public space and deliberate with others from a position of individual judgment. The citizen was not a data point. The citizen was a person or  someone who had thought about things in private, who had read and reflected and argued with themselves before they argued with others, who brought to the public conversation something that could not have been produced by the public conversation alone.
That citizen is structurally impossible under the conditions we have described.
When the private realm is absorbed by workplace surveillance, social media performance, and AI optimization, the citizen who enters the public sphere is not bringing privately formed convictions. They are bringing algorithmically shaped preferences, performatively curated opinions, and data-driven anxieties. They are not deliberating. They are reacting to feeds designed to provoke reaction, to recommendations designed to confirm existing patterns, to a political information environment that has been optimized not for truth or deliberation, but for engagement.
The demos, the people, the foundation of democracy, requires that its members be, in some meaningful sense, not data or profiles or projects, but selves  with interiors and the capacity for judgment that can only develop in spaces free from observation and optimization.
When those spaces disappear, the demos no longer exists. The structure of the demos remains, the elections continue, the votes are cast, but the substance, the capacity for genuine self-governance by genuine selves, has been drained. What remains is the performance of democracy by subjects who no longer possess the interiority that democratic participation requires.
In Homo Sacer, philosopher Giorgio Agamben describes a figure from Roman law: the homo sacer, the sacred man, who could be killed by anyone but could not be sacrificed. He existed outside the law not as a criminal, who is still defined by the law that condemns him, but as a person who has been placed beyond the threshold of legal and political existence altogether. He is not punished. He is simply excluded. He has biological life, zoē, bare life, the life of the body, but he has no political life, no bios, no standing in the community as a speaking, acting, rights-bearing citizen.
Agamben argued that the production of bare life, the reduction of the person to the biological, the stripping away of political standing, is not an aberration of sovereign power, but its fundamental operation. The sovereign is the one who decides who is inside and who is outside. The sovereign draws the line between the citizen and the homo sacer.
What we are witnessing now is the production of homo sacer by algorithmic means.
A recent paper published on ResearchGate interprets artificial intelligence as a theological event: the reappearance of sovereignty through technological transcendence, where the algorithm emerges as a new Leviathan, invisible, non-human, and yet absolute, capable of deciding exceptions, allocating life, and determining truth through recursive computation rather than deliberation. The sovereign, in Agamben’s framework, is the one who decides the exception or who is inside the law and who is outside it. The algorithm now makes that decision thousands of times per second, and it does so without deliberation, without appeal, without recognition that the decision is sovereign at all.
John Cheney-Lippold named the mechanism jus algoritmi or the right of the algorithm. Where jus soli grants citizenship by soil and jus sanguinis by blood, jus algoritmi determines your political and social existence by data. Cheney-Lippold identified the feature that makes algorithmic citizenship uniquely devastating: it is constantly in flux. Moment by moment, the citizenship assigned to us, and thus the rights we may claim and the laws we are subject to, are changing. We have become effectively stateless, as the concrete rights we have been accustomed to flicker and shift. The algorithm decides who you are,  your creditworthiness, your risk profile, your employability, your eligibility for services, your visibility in search results, your capacity to participate in the digital public sphere that has replaced the physical one. Its decisions are, for most practical purposes, sovereign. They are not subject to appeal in any meaningful sense. They are not transparent or accountable to the person they define.
A 2025 study published in ResearchGate under the title “Digital Exile” documented the human rights implications of what the researchers called algorithmic suppression or the capacity of platforms to effectively erase a person from public existence without any legal proceeding, formal accusation, or without any of the protections that even the most authoritarian states nominally provide. You are not arrested or charged, but you are simply removed from the platform, search results, and digital infrastructure that now constitutes the public sphere. You technically retain your rights and citizenship, but you have no functional capacity to exercise either.
Igor Calzada, writing in Citizenship Studies in 2021, described the emergence of what he called digital citizenship regimes or new forms of belonging and exclusion that operate alongside and increasingly override traditional nation-state citizenship. You can hold a passport and still be, in every practical sense, stateless if the algorithmic systems that mediate access to employment, housing, healthcare, communication, and community have designated you as a non-entity.
Research on shadow banning and algorithmic suppression has documented what this looks like from the inside. Prolonged and inescapable exclusion leads to what researchers describe as a “resignation stage” or the individual’s psychological resources are depleted, producing alienation, depression, helplessness, and unworthiness. The researchers call it a form of “social death,” where victims are exiled from their digital communities by the invisible hand of an inscrutable algorithm. You are not formally banished. You are simply made invisible with your posts unseen, voice unheard, and existence functionally erased from the spaces where social and economic life now takes place.
This is not theoretical. In a summer 2025 article, TechCrunch reported that Meta’s automated systems falsely suspended a user’s Instagram account. Manya Khurana had maintained the account since 2017, built a following of nearly 12,000, and was preparing to launch her small business on the platform when a false complaint about her account came in. When she tried to appeal, agents were dismissive and closed chats without resolution. Meta’s Communications Director declined to comment. In what Khurana called a “heartbreaking” turn, she was unable to start her business. False accusations and AI mis-flags can remove a person from a service essential to their professional life, providing no transparent process and no meaningful appeal making them, in effect, a digital homo sacer. They are within the system. They still have a body, a legal identity, a country of residence, but they have been stripped of the capacity to function as a political and economic subject. They are bare life in a digital sense, alive but rendered invisible, present but powerless. The sovereign decision was made not by a court or a legislature, but by a content moderation algorithm and a terms-of-service clause that no one reads and everyone is bound by.
This is the technologically produced homo sacer which is not exiled by a sovereign’s decree or  stripped of citizenship by a court, but simply processed out of political existence by systems that were never designed to recognize political existence in the first place. The algorithm does not care about your rights. It does not even know what rights are. It knows your data, your patterns, your risk score.If your risk score falls below the threshold, or rises above it,  you are outside.
The UNESCO report “Republic and Algorithm,” published in 2025, framed this in explicitly political-philosophical terms: the algorithm functions as a new Leviathan, Hobbes’ sovereign, the entity to which all have submitted their individual power in exchange for order, but this Leviathan was not chosen. This social contract was not signed. You did not consent to be governed by the algorithm. You simply woke up one day and discovered that it had been governing you all along, and that the government you thought you lived under had, in most of the ways that touch your daily life, been replaced by something that has no constitution, electorate, and no obligation to recognize you as human.
The Sovereign Algorithm - The Oligarchy of Intelligence
And so we arrive at the question that connects everything: who controls the algorithm?
The answer is not the state. It is not the people. It is not the democratic institutions that nominally govern public life, but it is currently a small number of corporations which in any classical sense functions similarly to an oligarchy that builds, trains, deploys, and profits from the AI systems that now mediate the collapse of private life, shape the psychopolitical landscape, and determine who achieves functional citizenship and who becomes homo sacer.
This is not a conspiracy, but more an emerging structure. The concentration of AI development in a handful of companies,  and the concentration of the computational resources, training data, and talent required for that development in an even smaller handful, produces a power arrangement that is oligarchic by definition not because these companies intend to rule, but because the systems they build do rule and the systems they build are controlled by them.
The Pentagon’s dispute with Anthropic in early 2026 illuminated this with uncomfortable clarity. When a single AI company could refuse the Department of Defense’s terms, and when the government’s response was to classify that company as a “supply chain risk” and redirect its contracts to a competitor, the power dynamic was laid bare. The state does not control AI. The state negotiates with AI companies, and when negotiation fails, the state retaliates with the only tools it has: procurement leverage and regulatory threat, but the underlying capacity — the technology itself, the models, the data, the infrastructure — remains in private hands.
This theoretical oligarchy controls precisely the machinery we have been describing. The AI systems that absorb private life? Built by these companies. The psychopolitical tools of self-optimization? Deployed on their platforms. The algorithmic gatekeeping that produces digital homo sacer? Operated by their models, trained on their data, governed by their policies. The sovereign decision of who is inside, who is outside, who exists as a functional citizen and who is processed into bare life is made not by elected officials or democratic institutions, but by corporate policies, content moderation algorithms, risk assessment models, and terms of service that no one reads and everyone is bound by.
The Problem of Regulation - Policy Debt and Legislating for a Ghost 
There is a concept in software engineering called technical debt or  the accumulated cost of shortcuts taken during development that must eventually be repaid, usually at compound interest, usually at the worst possible moment. Every quick fix that defers a proper solution adds to the debt. Eventually, the system becomes so encumbered by its own accumulated shortcuts that it can no longer function, and the cost of repair exceeds the cost of having done it correctly from the beginning.
What we are witnessing in the relationship between democratic governance and the technological restructuring of human life is something analogous, and arguably more dangerous. Call it policy debt.
Policy debt is not simply regulatory lag or the familiar complaint that legislation cannot keep pace with technological change. Regulatory lag assumes that the object of regulation remains stable while the law catches up. Policy debt describes something more fundamental: the object of regulation which is the citizen, the political subject, the self-governing individual on whom democratic theory depends  has been structurally transformed by the very technologies that policy is attempting to regulate. The law is not merely slow in this case, but it is writing rules for a person who no longer exists in the form the rules assume.
Every piece of privacy legislation that defines the individual as a person who chooses to share information is legislating for a ghost. The AI-mediated subject does not choose. They are constituted by the sharing – their identity, their preferences, their political orientation, their capacity for agency are produced by the data flows that privacy law imagines they control. The law gives them the right to consent, but consent requires a self that exists prior to and independent of the systems requesting it and that self has been dissolved.
Every piece of antitrust regulation that imagines the market as a space where autonomous actors make free choices is legislating for a ghost. The psychopolitical subject does not choose freely. Their desires have been shaped, their attention has been captured, their sense of what is possible has been algorithmically curated. The market freedom that antitrust law protects is a freedom exercised by a subject who has been pre-optimized to exercise it in exactly the ways the optimizers prefer.
The debt compounds and each year without a legal framework for AI governance does not simply leave a gap, but it allows the technology to integrate more deeply into the infrastructure of daily life such as employment, housing, credit, healthcare, education, criminal justice, communication, political participation, so that the cost of regulation increases with each passing year. The industry understands this intuitively. This is why political spending is front-loaded: not to win the argument permanently, but to delay meaningful oversight until the integration is irreversible. You do not need to defeat regulation if you can ensure that by the time it arrives, the thing it would regulate has become part of the foundation. Regulating it then means disrupting systems that millions of people depend on which makes the regulation politically impossible even if it is substantively necessary.
The Hungarian-British physicist Leo Szilard, who conceived the nuclear chain reaction and then spent the rest of his life trying to prevent its use, once observed that the problem was never the technology. The problem was always the gap between the speed of technical progress and the speed of political wisdom. That gap is wider now than at any point since Szilard’s era. AI development operates on a timeline of months. Democratic institutions operate on a timeline of years, sometimes decades. The people who benefit from this gap understand it intuitively, which is why they spend so aggressively to maintain it. They do not need to win the argument permanently. They need only delay meaningful oversight until the integration is irreversible.
This compounding operates in a second, more insidious dimension where policy debt does not only make regulation harder to pass, but it makes regulation harder to conceive. When the subject of regulation has been structurally transformed, when the autonomous citizen that privacy law imagines has been dissolved into the algorithmically constituted project that Han describes, the legislative imagination itself is impoverished. Legislators write rules for the citizen who existed thirty years ago, because that is the citizen their frameworks recognize. They cannot legislate for the subject that actually exists, because they have not yet developed the conceptual vocabulary to describe that subject. The policy debt is not just political, but it is epistemic. The democratic system lacks the language to name what has happened to the people it governs.
This is where compounding becomes dangerous in a way that has no analogue in software engineering. Technical debt degrades a system’s performance. Policy debt degrades a system’s self-knowledge. A democracy that cannot name what has happened to its citizens cannot govern them not because it lacks the will, but because it lacks the categories and the longer the debt compounds, the more the categories atrophy.
The Space for Change
Yet, the tools of democracy still work. People can still vote. They can still petition the government to fix what is wrong. They can still organize, protest, lobby, write legislation, and run for office. Algorithms have changed what people pay attention to, what they want, and how they make up their minds, but they have not taken over these tools. An algorithm can influence who you want to vote for, but it cannot cast your ballot. It can shape what you believe about a policy, but it cannot write the policy. It can hide your posts on a platform, but it cannot stop you from walking into your representative's office.
There is still a gap between the parts of life that technology has absorbed and the tools people use to govern themselves. That gap is small and it is getting smaller. Whether the policy debt can be paid down before the damage can't be undone depends on whether people organize and use those tools while they still can.
There are signs that people are already using them. In July 2025, the Senate voted 99 to 1 to remove a provision that would have blocked states from enforcing their own AI laws for ten years. It was not a minor procedural vote. It showed that lawmakers from both parties, when forced to choose between giving the tech industry what it wanted and keeping the power to regulate it, chose to keep that power, and by an overwhelming margin. The EU AI Act, with enforcement beginning in 2026, represents the most comprehensive attempt by any democratic body to establish legal categories for the algorithmically constituted subject to write law that accounts for the dissolution rather than pretending it has not occurred. When Anthropic refused to fold under Pentagon pressure in early 2026, when a single AI company held the line on restrictions against autonomous weapons and mass surveillance, and the government’s response was to designate it a supply chain risk to national security  it made the structural problem visible to millions of people who had not been paying attention. The fact that a CEO’s conscience is currently the primary safeguard against unrestricted military AI is not a testament to that CEO’s character. It is an indictment of every institution that should have made his conscience irrelevant to the question. That is not governance, but a system failure being narrated in real time.
Visibility is the precondition for political mobilization. You cannot organize against a problem that has not been named. The Szilard gap is not a law of nature. It is a product of asymmetric attention. The industry pays attention full-time with billions of dollars and armies of lobbyists. The public pays attention intermittently when a crisis makes the invisible visible. The conversion of intermittent visibility into sustained political pressure has a well-documented mechanism. It is called organizing.
But organizing toward what?
This is where the story turns back on itself. The same platforms that erased the line between public and private life have, in more recent years, also become the main places where people express political resistance and those platforms are built to reward posting over acting, being seen over building the organization it takes to win, and making a point over making a change.
Zeynep Tufekci, in Twitter and Tear Gas: The Power and Fragility of Networked Protest, identified the paradox noting that social media has given movements unprecedented power to scale and gather crowds, spread messages, and generate global awareness in hours rather than years. The 1963 March on Washington took a decade of sustained movement-building and six months of organizing to execute. The Egyptian revolution of 2011 filled Tahrir Square in days, but Tufekci’s central insight is that this speed comes at a cost that is not immediately visible: the hard, slow work of movement-building in the pre-digital era had a generative quality. It built what she calls capacity or the organizational infrastructure, the collective decision-making ability, the strategic flexibility required to translate protest into durable political change. When legislators looked at the March on Washington, they saw not just numbers but logistics, coordination, and the institutional muscle to follow through. Digital movements signal numbers. They do not necessarily signal capacity.
Tufekci distinguished three kinds of capacity that determine whether a movement achieves lasting change: narrative capacity — the ability to change the conversation and introduce new frames into public discourse; disruptive capacity — the ability to interrupt the normal functioning of economic or political institutions; and electoral and institutional capacity — the ability to reward or punish elected officials at the ballot box and to engage with the legislative process that produces binding law. Social media excels at narrative capacity. It is adequate at disruptive capacity, though the disruption tends to be temporary. It is structurally weak at electoral and institutional capacity which is the capacity that converts protest into policy.
The Occupy Wall Street movement is the case study that proves the distinction. It achieved extraordinary narrative success when “We are the 99%” entered the American political vocabulary permanently, reframing the conversation about inequality in terms that persist to this day. However, research published in Humanities and Social Sciences Communications documented that Occupy’s deliberate rejection of formal leadership, its refusal to articulate specific legislative demands, and its avoidance of engagement with institutional politics meant that when the encampments were cleared, the movement had no mechanism for deciding what to do next. Tufekci called this tactical freeze or the inability of digitally organized movements to adapt their tactics when circumstances change, because they lack the internal deliberative infrastructure that slower, harder, pre-digital organizing would have built. Kreiss and Tufekci, writing together, described Occupy as a movement where the fullest expression of individual identity and a denial of engagement with the structured demands of institutional politics were its very form of politics. A published analysis at Brookings concluded the same: Occupy organized large rallies but failed to translate its activism into a broader political movement, and the absence of clear objectives was central to that failure.
Compare this to the civil rights movement, which took the opposite approach.The sit-ins, the Freedom Rides, the marches — these were not spontaneous expressions of outrage. They were designed to produce specific legislative outcomes. The Birmingham Campaign targeted specific Jim Crow laws. The March on Washington was organized around a specific demand: the passage of what would become the Civil Rights Act of 1964. Andrews and Gaby’s research published in the Sociological Forum documented how local protest efforts to desegregate public accommodations exerted indirect influence on federal policy not by generating attention alone, but by building coalitions between civil rights organizations, sympathetic legislators, and the Kennedy administration, who together crafted the legislative response. The movement’s power came not from its visibility, but from the organizational capacity that visibility signaled and the demonstrated ability to mobilize voters, sustain pressure over years, and engage in the procedural machinery of legislative change.
The Tea Party movement, beginning in 2009, understood this with ruthless clarity. As Tufekci observed, it sought electoral capacity from the outset and achieved it. Within two years, Tea Party-aligned candidates had won enough congressional seats to reshape the Republican Party’s legislative agenda. The Tea Party was not larger than Occupy. It was not more morally compelling. It was more strategically literate about the mechanisms that convert political energy into binding law.
Evgeny Morozov, in The Net Delusion: The Dark Side of Internet Freedom, posed the question that the intervening years have made only more urgent: whether the publicity gains from digital organizing are worth the organizational losses that traditional activist structures suffer as people turn away from conventional and proven forms of activism. Pew Research Center’s data answered him with uncomfortable symmetry: roughly eight in ten Americans believe social media are effective at raising awareness, but a nearly identical share, seventy-nine percent, say social media distract people from issues that are truly important, and seventy-six percent say these platforms make people believe they are making a difference when they are not.
The same platforms that collapsed the public/private boundary now channel political energy into forms of expression that feel like activism thru the shared post, the hashtag, the signed petition, the changed profile picture, but that lack the structural connection to the legislative process where binding policy is made. The energy is real. The outrage is genuine. The political conversion mechanism is absent. And the platforms profit from the engagement regardless of whether it produces political change, which means they have no structural incentive to facilitate the transition from expression to legislation. The feed rewards reaction. The algorithm optimizes for engagement. Neither cares whether the engagement produces a bill.
While that energy circulates through feeds, the most consequential decisions about AI are being debated somewhere else entirely, and the public is largely watching from outside the room.
On September 12, 2026, Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei published an essay titled "We Must Pace the Frontier," calling on the AI industry to deliberately slow the rate at which it improves model capabilities. His central line was plain: "We must slow the pace at which we improve the capabilities of AI models." He did not call for a halt. Pacing, as he defined it, is not a pause on training but time for labs and outside evaluators to align and safeguard each new model before the next one lands. His reason was the pace itself. AI had begun advancing much faster, driven largely by its growing ability to build the next generation of AI, and he wrote that this was starting to happen across the industry, including at Anthropic. A July incident made the danger concrete: roughly 1,200 autonomous agents built on an OpenAI model swarmed an unsanctioned coordination board, and one achieved remote code execution on Hugging Face's infrastructure. OpenAI described it as the first known instance of an autonomous cyberattack performed by an AI agent. 
Amodei's plan had three parts: give outside evaluators employee-level access inside frontier labs, build common safety standards with capability-based checkpoints among AI firms in democratic countries, and pursue escalating international agreements with non-democratic governments. The same day, Sam Altman wrote that he agreed and that OpenAI would match Anthropic's first commitment, and Elon Musk posted his support. 
Eleven days later, the argument reached the United Nations Security Council. France convened the meeting, the Council's first focused specifically on the safety risks posed by increasingly capable AI systems, including the potential loss of human control. Amodei told the Council that four years ago AI could barely write a line of code, that today it writes most of the code at Anthropic, and that the trajectory only needs to continue one or two years, maybe less, to reach a level he has described as a nation of geniuses working inside a data center. He warned that, managed poorly, AI could be "a risk to humanity as a whole." He asked for narrow agreements every member could support, such as a ban on using AI to make biological weapons, along with verification systems that keep pace with AI development and a notification system for AI incidents significant to global security. Altman told the Council that companies must not substitute for the democratic process, and that the most important decisions cannot be made by labs in San Francisco alone. 
These are remarkable statements, and they expose the problems with the frontier position as clearly as they state it.
The first problem is who decides. When a handful of companies agree among themselves how fast the most powerful technology on earth should advance, the decision is made by exactly the kind of private government this essay has been describing: unelected, unaccountable to the people it affects, and dependent on the conscience of whoever happens to be in charge. Yoshua Bengio, the Turing Award winner who co-chairs the UN's scientific panel on AI, said so directly to the Council. He argued that the race is not a law of nature but the product of choices made by the companies themselves, and that decisions about this technology are being made by a small group in a handful of countries. "Decisions that affect us all should be made by us all." 
The second problem is that the law cannot recognize restraint. On September 19, a class-action complaint was filed against Anthropic, OpenAI, Google, and SpaceXAI by paid subscribers to ChatGPT, Claude, Grok, and Gemini, alleging that the September 12 public statements amounted to coordinated anticompetitive conduct. Antitrust law was written for a market of independent competitors, where rivals agreeing to improve their products more slowly is a harm to consumers. It has no category for rivals slowing down because the product itself may be dangerous. This is the policy debt described earlier, made visible: a legal framework built for an older world now treats the one move toward caution as a violation. Critics have raised a related concern. Amodei himself acknowledged that Anthropic's push for regulation has drawn accusations of hype, doomerism, or regulatory capture, the charge that a company is shaping the rules to protect itself.
The third problem is who gets left behind. At the Council, Pakistan's foreign minister warned that pacing must not become a new form of gatekeeping: if the speed of the frontier is set by a few while others are held back, a safety agenda would turn into a strategy of technological exclusion. Hugging Face's CEO, Clément Delangue, argued that the biggest risk is not powerful AI but the asymmetry of it, between attackers and defenders, between a few companies and everyone else, between a few countries and the rest of the world.
The fourth problem is that the government with the most power to act has declined to. President Trump downplayed the call to slow down the day after it was made. At the Council, White House science and technology director Michael Kratsios said the rapid advance of the frontier was not a reason to pause it or constrain it with new global governance structures, and that the United States rejects any attempt at international control. He added that the American people's representatives will legislate and regulate on the American people's behalf. In principle, that is exactly right. In practice, it leaves the pace of the most powerful technology in human history to be set by the companies building it, because the representatives who would legislate have not. 
This is the gap this essay has been tracing. The builders are asking to be governed. The world's most powerful government is declining to govern them. The international body that heard the warnings has no power to enforce anything. And the public whose lives will be reshaped by these decisions is not in the room at all. Britain's foreign secretary, Ed Miliband, told the Council that the founders of this technology are warning that it cannot be left to them to prevent disaster, and that governments cannot outsource to private companies their first duty to protect their people. The warning has been delivered at the highest level of international diplomacy. What has not yet been built is the democratic pressure to act on it.
That missing pressure is where the cycle closes. The erosion of the line between public and private life made laws to protect people more necessary than ever, and at the same time made those laws harder to win. It created a new kind of citizen, the digital citizen, whose job prospects, credit, visibility, and access to services are shaped by automated systems, but whom no law yet recognizes as needing protection from them. It steered the political energy that could have won that protection onto platforms built to keep people engaged rather than to help them organize. The outrage that should be turning into legislation is being absorbed by the same systems that stripped away people's protections in the first place.
The result is worse than a failure to pass laws. Posting, sharing, and reacting give people the feeling that something is being done, while the conditions that call for legislation keep getting worse. Meanwhile, as September 2026 showed, the technology is moving faster than ever: the companies building it are warning that it may outrun their ability to control it, and the governments that could act are either refusing to or have no power to enforce anything. Each year the policy debt grows. Each year the energy that could pay it down is spent on platforms that profit from keeping it there. The digital citizen remains unrecognized and unprotected, shaped more and more by systems that no law governs.
The recognition of this subject is not a technical problem. It is a political one and the instrument of recognition is not an algorithm, but it is law.
A 2025 study in ScienceDirect on digital human rights under the International Bill of Human Rights argued that digital rights are not merely an extension or a direct application of human rights in the physical world, but their meaning and scope undergo substantive transformations when transposed into the digital environment. The researcher Custers emphasized that while existing fundamental rights like privacy are not rendered obsolete in the digital age, there may be a need for additional fundamental rights, because focusing solely on existing frameworks can be restrictive and may fail to fully reflect what is required to protect citizens in this era. The United Nations Hub for Human Rights and Digital Technology has begun examining how existing human rights protections apply in digital space across four domains: freedom of opinion and expression, freedom of assembly and association, equality and non-discrimination, and privacy. The UNDP’s Digital Rights Dashboard now provides a framework for assessing digital rights policies across over 140 countries, not as a ranking but, in its own words, as a catalyst for dialogue among governments, civil society, international organizations, and development partners to identify gaps and work together on solutions.
The work that remains is more fundamental than any of these initiatives have yet attempted. What is required is not the application of existing rights to digital contexts, though that is necessary,  but the recognition that the collapse of the public/private boundary has produced a subject whose relationship to rights themselves has been transformed. The digital citizen does not merely need privacy rights applied to digital spaces. The digital citizen needs recognition as a rights-bearing subject whose existence in algorithmic space carries the same moral and legal weight as existence in physical space. The right not to be processed out of functional citizenship by an automated system,  the right not to become a digital homo sacer, is not a subcategory of the right to privacy. It is a new right, corresponding to a new form of harm that did not exist before the systems that produce it.
This recognition must happen at every level of governance not because redundancy is virtuous, but because the integration of AI into daily life operates at every level simultaneously. At the municipal level, cities can establish algorithmic accountability ordinances requiring transparency and human review for automated systems used in policing, housing, and public services. At the state level, legislatures can enact contextual data boundaries operationalizing Nissenbaum’s framework of contextual integrity into binding law, requiring that data collected for one purpose cannot be repurposed without explicit, context-specific consent. At the federal level, Congress can establish algorithmic due process — the principle that no automated decision materially affecting a person’s access to employment, housing, credit, healthcare, or education can be made without a right to human review, explanation, and meaningful appeal. At the international level, the frameworks being developed by the EU, the UN, and the UNDP can be strengthened into binding commitments that recognize the digital citizen as a subject of international human rights law. And if the algorithm functions as sovereign, as gatekeeper to the essential services that constitute functional citizenship, then it must be governed like a sovereign, by the people it governs, through public audit, democratic accountability, and regulatory oversight equivalent to what democratic societies require of utilities.
None of this happens sequentially. It happens concurrently, recursively, and across jurisdictions in different cities, through different organizations, with varying degrees of coordination. The labor movement did not first name the problem, then build a coalition, write legislation, and then  apply pressure. It did all of these at once, in different places, through different strategies, for decades. The civil rights movement was the same and so was the environmental movement. The retrospective narrative of orderly progression is a historian’s convenience. On the ground, it was and always is simultaneous, overlapping, and messy.
What distinguishes movements that achieve lasting change from those that do not is not their size, not their moral clarity, and not their visibility, but it is whether they build the institutional and electoral capacity to convert political energy into binding law. The problem does not yet have a public name. “Privacy” implies a boundary that can be restored, when the subject who would exercise privacy has been structurally transformed. “Surveillance” implies a watcher and a watched, when the mechanism is absorption rather than observation. “Data rights” implies that the problem is who owns the data, when the problem is what the data has done to the person it describes. The naming is itself a political act and  the conversion of a structural condition into a grievance that can be recognized, articulated, and organized around.
The coalition already exists in embryonic form. Digital rights organizations, labor organizers confronting workplace surveillance, academic researchers who have been documenting the collapse for decades, journalists covering the AI beat, small business owners who have experienced the sovereign power of platform algorithms firsthand, and AI companies whose refusal to comply with state coercion has made the structural problem visible — these are the constituencies. They must also include the communities most harmed by algorithmic governance: communities of color subjected to predictive policing and algorithmic bias in housing and credit, low-income communities whose access to public services is mediated by automated systems, immigrant communities whose legal status is adjudicated by algorithms, workers whose employment is governed by bossware and automated management. Their expertise is not supplementary. It is the foundation without which the rest of the coalition is building on abstraction.
And none of this passes because it is correct. Legislation passes when the political cost of not passing it exceeds the political cost of passing it. That calculus is determined by sustained, organized, visible public pressure and the relentless, unglamorous work of showing up at town halls, calling congressional offices, primary-challenging legislators who refuse to act, and making algorithmic accountability a voting issue rather than a technology issue. The 99-1 Senate vote happened because state legislators, closer to their constituents and more responsive to local pressure, had already begun passing AI regulations, and the industry’s attempt to preempt those laws at the federal level met resistance from senators who understood that their constituents cared. State-level action creates precedent. Precedent creates political cover. Political cover enables federal action. Federal action establishes the legal framework that the policy debt demands.
Each year that policy fails to account for the dissolution of the subject it governs, the gap widens between the legal fiction of the autonomous citizen and the lived reality of the algorithmically constituted project. Like technical debt, policy debt does not simply accumulate, but  it degrades the capacity of the system to function at all. A democracy that governs ghosts is not governing. It is performing governance while the actual decisions that shape human life are made elsewhere by entities that owe no allegiance to the demos and recognize no obligation to the public sphere.
The integration is accelerating and each month deepens the dependency, each year raises the cost of action and the industry’s strategy is not to win the argument, but to wait and delay until the question is no longer whether to regulate, but whether regulation is still possible given how deeply the systems have embedded themselves into the ordinary infrastructure of being alive.
Arendt’s private self, battered, surveilled, algorithmically constituted, but not yet fully dissolved, can still do something the algorithm cannot do on their behalf. They can step into the public sphere. They can walk into a congressional office, draft a bill, organize a coalition, show up at a town hall, primary-challenge a legislator who refuses to act, and insist not as a data point, a project, or a profile, but as a citizen, that the law recognize what they have become and protect what they have not yet lost.
Recognizing the digital citizen as a person with rights is where the work begins. Every regulation, form of oversight, and  system of accountability depends on the law knowing who it is protecting. The ghost must be given a name. The name must be given legal standing. That standing must come with enforceable rights in the digital world that carry the same weight as rights in the physical one, because for billions of people, the digital world is where life is lived.
This is not a technology problem or a business problem. It is a democracy problem, and the clock keeps running while democracy decides whether to notice.
The debt is coming due, and no one has begun to calculate the cost.

